@Steve L
I mostly agree with you but after all it's just one tap to sort. And as a semiretired person who enjoys checking the comments more than once per day, I find it handy to see recent first on my later visits. 
  Never ceases to amaze me that so many people aren't at least ATAD ABASHED to say exactly what has already been said and sometimes more than once. Why not just Recommend? Or Reply if one actually has something to add?

@RozzieGrandma,

Hear, hear!

(I also sort comments by oldest first, and I heartily agree that it should be the default for when we first enter into them)

@Mean Old Lady

In a situation where you may say "Buzz off!", others might say "BITE ME!"

(My wife is partial to the latter.)

@Matt
My observations are that people are more likely to complain that M-W puzzles are too hard. For a while I've noticed that  T, and now M, have been Toughened Up, a good thing as far as I'm concerned, and that Wednesday has turned tricky some weeks. 
Most weeks Thursdays, Fridays and Saturdays continue to be easier than in the past, but I'm just tired of dwelling on it.

@Matt

I'm 71, and the only one in your example that I didn't know right off was DARIA.  I've never been a TV watcher, so I have trouble with that type of clue in any timeframe.  I've been a long time Eurythmics (and Tourists) fan, and the other two I likely learned from puzzles.

As for The Easening in general, in the past few days, I just finished a random month of Saturdays from 2016.  With the exception of one, I found them far more difficult than anything I've seen in the NYT in years.  If you have time on your hands and feel like doing some extra puzzling, I'd be curious to know how you felt about them.

@sonnel
Not only did Mendelssohn's "songs without words" not have words, most of them didn't have titles, either. Although Mendelssohn did title a handful of them ("Venetian Boat Song," "Duetto"), but most of the "titles" were added by editors and publishers. Here's Op. 38, No. 1, which Constantin von Sternberg and G. Schirmer sounded like "the Evening Star"--frankly, I don't hear it:
<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9cDVO9MEEMg&list=RD9cDVO9MEEMg&start_radio=1" target="_blank">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9cDVO9MEEMg&list=RD9cDVO9MEEMg&start_radio=1</a>

@AnnMarie Rowland,

But it does sound like the start of a nonsense poem:

“I saw Esau … “ da da da da da.

@SP
Oh sorry I misread your post—I thought you meant you wished they would stop having old lady clues.
You can take your tongue out of your cheek now, I got you.

@Steve L that makes sense. I am very used to seeing acronyms and single capital letters being pluralised where it is easily distinguished between word and acronym/letter. CPUs for example, and a sentence such as 'I have three Rs'. 

In this situation it is explicitly the lower case x and y that are commonly associated with algebra so it makes sense to use 'x's and y's' for the cross word clue rather than Xs and Ys. 

Always learning. Thank you!

